Z||000006||turbo86se||06-12-2001||12:53 PM||||I've always wandered if my '88 coupe V6 could tow a 19' Bayliner. I'm thinking about getting one soon. That would be a sight to see! I don't know about loading out of the water!<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000007||ray b||06-12-2001||01:16 PM||||i would use a trailer with brakes if over 1000 lbs, dual axel helps to keep tonge weight down.<BR>winch the boat out of the water to save the car, mount it on  the trailer and use a dolly on the hitch [small wheels] to pull trailer up ramp to the car plus you can use the same winch to get the boat on the trailer.<BR>i lived on a boat more years than on land<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000008||Ken Wittlief||06-12-2001||02:11 PM||||The owners manual says the max towing capicity for any fiero is 1000 lbs.<P> you can pull more than this in a pinch. you can get away with lots of stuff if you only do it once (like driving with 5 people in your fiero).<P>  but you are taking a risk. under normal conditions nothing bad will happen. but if you have to lock up the brakes, swerve to avoid an accident, get into a curve too fast, then there will be a smear on the bedrock wall on the side of the road, where you use to be, and a little cross of flowers that your loved ones will put there every year to remember you.<P>  If you goto uhaul or any other such place to rent a trailer, they will ask you what you are gonna put in it. If the total weight is more that 1000 lbs and you are pulling it with a fiero, they wont give you the trailer.<P>  generally speaking, when towing anything, you want the tow vehilce to weigh twice as much as everything you are pulling.<P>  If you are only gonna need to do it once, then rent a truck to tow the trailer with, or put the formula Vee in the truck, borrow an F350 from somebody, a full size van, something that weighs about 4000 lbs.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Ken Wittlief||06-12-2001||02:17 PM||||if you will be carting the thing around alot, you can get a high milage full size van for a reasonable price, and have room to store tools and parts, and even a place to crash overnight if you need to.<P>  you should be able to get one that will run 'good enough' for $2000 or so.<P>  Its best if you put the extra stuff in the van instead of the trailor. Weight in the trailor pushes you forward when you want to stop - weight in the van also pushes you forward, but it also pushed the tires down on the road harder too, giving you better braking and steering than you would have if all the extra weight is back in the trailor.<P>  And BTW you definately want brakes on a trailor with a gross weight of 2000 lbs.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Steve Normington||06-12-2001||02:34 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by turbo86se:<BR><B>I've always wandered if my '88 coupe V6 could tow a 19' Bayliner. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You can mount the 5th wheel on the strut towers.<BR>||||reg||1
